WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.362
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level one.

2
00:00:05.662 --> 00:00:09.071
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.371 --> 00:00:16.575
Unit thirteen: Investigative Journalism. Today you will report allegations with reporting verbs and noun clauses.

4
00:00:16.875 --> 00:00:19.550
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:24.209
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:26.225
Whistleblower.

7
00:00:27.217 --> 00:00:31.294
Whistleblower means a person who reveals wrongdoing inside an organisation.

8
00:00:31.883 --> 00:00:34.278
A whistleblower contacted the newspaper.

9
00:00:34.883 --> 00:00:37.721
Your turn. Say: Whistleblower.

10
00:00:40.567 --> 00:00:41.672
Whistleblower.

11
00:00:42.417 --> 00:00:43.952
Confidential source.

12
00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:48.830
Confidential source means a person whose name a reporter keeps secret.

13
00:00:49.417 --> 00:00:52.043
A confidential source gave the documents.

14
00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:56.278
Your turn. Say: Confidential source.

15
00:00:59.133 --> 00:01:00.622
Confidential source.

16
00:01:01.383 --> 00:01:02.466
Document leak.

17
00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:07.284
Document leak means the secret release of private papers to the press.

18
00:01:07.883 --> 00:01:10.452
A document leak revealed the secret contract.

19
00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:14.051
Your turn. Say: Document leak.

20
00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:18.075
Document leak.

21
00:01:18.817 --> 00:01:19.980
Fact-checking.

22
00:01:20.983 --> 00:01:24.701
Fact-checking means the process of confirming that information is true.

23
00:01:25.317 --> 00:01:27.258
Fact-checking took three weeks.

24
00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:30.317
Your turn. Say: Fact-checking.

25
00:01:33.167 --> 00:01:34.864
Fact-checking.

26
00:01:35.617 --> 00:01:36.873
Public interest.

27
00:01:37.883 --> 00:01:41.369
Public interest means the welfare of the general public.

28
00:01:41.983 --> 00:01:44.180
The story was in the public interest.

29
00:01:44.783 --> 00:01:47.726
Your turn. Say: Public interest.

30
00:01:50.567 --> 00:01:51.800
Public interest.

31
00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:53.795
Accountability.

32
00:01:54.783 --> 00:01:58.199
Accountability means being responsible for your actions.

33
00:01:58.783 --> 00:02:01.700
Journalism demands accountability from leaders.

34
00:02:02.317 --> 00:02:04.830
Your turn. Say: Accountability.

35
00:02:07.667 --> 00:02:09.039
Accountability.

36
00:02:09.783 --> 00:02:10.901
Exposé.

37
00:02:11.917 --> 00:02:15.437
Exposé means a news story that reveals something shocking.

38
00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:17.875
The exposé shocked the nation.

39
00:02:18.483 --> 00:02:21.240
Your turn. Say: Exposé.

40
00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:25.206
Exposé.

41
00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:27.346
Follow-up story.

42
00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:32.091
Follow-up story means a later story that adds new information.

43
00:02:32.683 --> 00:02:34.962
A follow-up story appeared on Monday.

44
00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:38.667
Your turn. Say: Follow-up story.

45
00:02:41.533 --> 00:02:43.742
Follow-up story.

46
00:02:44.533 --> 00:02:48.219
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:49.083 --> 00:02:53.651
Visal, an exposé claims that a company hid pollution. How does the paper know?

48
00:02:54.750 --> 00:03:04.215
A whistleblower and a confidential source gave documents, and after long fact-checking the reporters confirmed that the claim was true.

49
00:03:05.317 --> 00:03:07.514
I notice 'claims that' and 'revealed that'.

50
00:03:08.617 --> 00:03:18.221
Reporting verbs show how sure the paper is: claim and allege are cautious, while reveal and confirm mean the facts are checked.

51
00:03:19.317 --> 00:03:20.898
Why take such a risk?

52
00:03:21.983 --> 00:03:28.618
Public interest and accountability. A follow-up story can then show whether leaders respond.

53
00:03:29.767 --> 00:03:31.430
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:32.283 --> 00:03:36.454
Grammar focus: Reporting verbs: claim, allege, reveal.

55
00:03:36.754 --> 00:03:37.987
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:38.683 --> 00:03:41.635
The whistleblower claims that the company hid its pollution.

57
00:03:42.550 --> 00:03:45.200
The leak reveals that the contract was secret.

58
00:03:46.083 --> 00:03:48.861
The paper alleges that officials ignored the warnings.

59
00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:57.561
Claim and allege are cautious; reveal and confirm show checked facts. Always add 'that' for clarity: claims that the company hid it.

60
00:03:58.317 --> 00:04:01.967
Be careful! Don't say: The paper claim the firm lied.

61
00:04:03.933 --> 00:04:06.525
Say: The paper claims that the firm lied.

62
00:04:06.825 --> 00:04:08.941
Add -s after a singular subject.

63
00:04:09.733 --> 00:04:12.397
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:14.782
Behind the Exposé.

65
00:04:15.082 --> 00:04:18.498
Investigative journalism can take months of careful work.

66
00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:25.157
A whistleblower and a confidential source may give a reporter secret papers after a document leak.

67
00:04:26.167 --> 00:04:31.719
Fact-checking then shows whether the documents are real, and the paper alleges only what it can support.

68
00:04:32.733 --> 00:04:38.227
If the exposé reveals that wrongdoing happened, public interest demands a follow-up story.

69
00:04:39.233 --> 00:04:42.069
In this way, accountability becomes possible.

70
00:04:43.167 --> 00:04:47.180
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:48.017 --> 00:04:50.759
The whistleblower blank that the company hid pollution.

72
00:04:51.059 --> 00:04:54.829
A, claims. B, claiming. C, claim to.

73
00:04:58.367 --> 00:05:00.471
The answer is A: claims.

74
00:05:01.617 --> 00:05:04.185
Confirming that information is true is blank.

75
00:05:04.485 --> 00:05:08.208
A, fact-checking. B, a leak. C, publication.

76
00:05:11.767 --> 00:05:13.952
The answer is A: fact-checking.

77
00:05:15.117 --> 00:05:19.055
A person who reveals wrongdoing inside an organisation is a blank.

78
00:05:19.355 --> 00:05:22.846
A, critic. B, whistleblower. C, consumer.

79
00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:28.609
The answer is B: whistleblower.

80
00:05:29.750 --> 00:05:31.959
Which verb shows the facts are checked?

81
00:05:32.259 --> 00:05:36.202
A, allege. B, reveal. C, might claim.

82
00:05:39.767 --> 00:05:41.906
The answer is B: reveal.

83
00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:46.338
According to the reading, a follow-up story is needed when…

84
00:05:46.638 --> 00:05:51.894
A, wrongdoing is revealed. B, nothing happens. C, the paper wishes.

85
00:05:55.433 --> 00:05:58.257
The answer is A: wrongdoing is revealed.

86
00:05:59.467 --> 00:06:02.874
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:03.717 --> 00:06:08.003
Why do journalists sometimes protect the identity of a confidential source?

88
00:06:16.817 --> 00:06:18.387
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:18.687 --> 00:06:40.779
Journalists protect a confidential source because the person may face danger or lose a job. If sources were named, few people would dare to reveal wrongdoing. Protecting them therefore helps the public learn the truth. The reporter must still check the facts carefully, because a secret source can be mistaken.

90
00:06:41.079 --> 00:06:42.742
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:43.650 --> 00:06:49.042
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and reporting verbs: claim, allege, reveal.

92
00:06:49.342 --> 00:06:53.736
Today's value is moral courage. Do what is right even when it is difficult.

93
00:06:54.036 --> 00:06:56.665
See you in Unit fourteen. Goodbye!

94
00:06:56.965 --> 00:07:00.221
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

